-- title:
--   Tate point of the H₁ problem reads j(q^{qd})
-- statement:
--   The setting is the following. A prime $q$ and a natural number $M'$ with $M' \neq 0$ and $q \nmid M'$ are fixed, together with a prime $\ell_g$ satisfying $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$.
--
--   A first group of data concerns the reduction at $q$ of the full modular function field. It consists of a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `hA : A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the non-units of $A$; a finite set $W$ of places of the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A, M') = \mathrm{ResidueField}(A)(j, j_{M'})$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, with `hW` saying that $W$ is exactly the set `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)` of supersingular places; the inclusion `hle` of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full level-$M'$ modular function field, in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, the base change of the function field of $X_H(q^2M')$ for $H = \mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M' = \ker(\mathrm{ZMod.unitsMap})$; a constant reduction $R_0$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A, M')$ relative to $A$ (a valuation subring of integers, a surjective residue map with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility with $A$ and with $\mathrm{residue}_A$, a scaling clause, and a place map preserving degrees and order functions); the hypothesis `hR₀`, which says that for a Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-coefficients lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that element is in $R_0$.integers and its $R_0$-residue, viewed as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$; and a chosen element $s$ of $W$.
--
--   A second group of data is the small arithmetic base: an intermediate field $k_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ and an element $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lying in $A$, such that $A_0 := A \cap k_0$ (formally the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$) is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ (`hunif`), is Henselian local (`hhens`), has algebraically closed residue field (`hres`), and is such that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$ (`hκ`).
--
--   A third group fixes roots of unity and the level field: an element $\xi \in k_0$ which is a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity (`hξ`), together with the existence of an embedding $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell_g)}$ (`hιξ`); a subgroup $H_1$ of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ equal to $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M' \cap \ker(\mathrm{ZMod.unitsMap})$ for the reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$ (`hH₁`); an intermediate field $K$ of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ over $k_0$ equal to the Laurent base change to $k_0$ of the function field $\mathrm{xHFunctionField}(q^2M', H_1)$ (`hK`), an $A_0$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $k_0$; and an element $j \in K$ whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of $\mathrm{jq}$ (`hj`), with $j \neq 0$.
--
--   A fourth group provides the equivariance axioms needed to build the rigid moduli datum over $A_0$: `hℓ` (variable changes preserve $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-points, i.e. the conditions that $(x_P,y_P)$ satisfies the Weierstrass equation, that $x_P$ is a root of $\mathrm{preΨ}\,\ell_g$, and that $(x_Q,y_Q) = (x_P,y_P)$), `hM` (variable changes carry $\mathrm{IsGamma0PowAt}\,W\,p\,k\,h$ to $\mathrm{IsGamma0PowAt}$ for the transformed kernel polynomial $\mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}$ in degree $\mathrm{gamma0PowDeg}\,p\,k$), and `hL` (divisibility of $\mathrm{inLineMulPoly}$ is preserved under variable changes). Further, group laws $\mathcal{G}$ on projective Weierstrass models over $A_0$-algebras with `h𝒢 : 𝒢.IsChordTangent` and `h𝒢O : 𝒢.IsOriginIdentity`, a level transport $\mathcal{T}$ for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$ with `h𝒯 : 𝒯.IsSectionTransport`, and the two existence hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO`, which produce graded ring maps realising variable changes, respectively coefficient maps, on the graded rings $\mathrm{projModelGradingCR}$ of projective Weierstrass models, together with the required inclusion of irrelevant ideals.
--
--   With these, $\mathcal{R} := \mathrm{rigidDataH1Pow}(A_0, \ell_g, M', q, h_\ell, h_M, h_L, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{T})$ is the rigid Weierstrass datum whose points over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ are equivalence classes of raw data consisting of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant, a $\Gamma_0(M')$-tuple of kernel polynomials, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point and a Drinfeld level-$q$ pair, subject to the link condition. The fifth group of data: a fine moduli package $P_0$ for $\mathcal{R}.\mathrm{toLevelModuliDatum}$, i.e. an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ with a universal point and the property that every point over any $A_0$-algebra is induced by a unique $A_0$-algebra map from $B_0$; a point $x_0$ of $\mathcal{R}$ over $K$ whose $j$-invariant is $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,q$ as a Laurent series (`hx₀`); an $A_0$-algebra map $\mathrm{clC}$ from $B_0$ to the finite chart algebra $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K\,j$ whose composition with the inclusion into $K$ agrees with $P_0.\mathrm{classify}\,x_0$ (`hclC`).
--
--   The final hypothesis `hμ` pins $x_0$ at the Tate point. It asserts the existence of a variable change $C$ over $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ and of a raw representative $r$ of $x_0$ such that: the class of $r$ equals $x_0$; writing $\mathsf{q}$ for the unit attached to $\xi$ and $(X,Y) = \mathrm{tateToricPoint}\,k_0\,q\,(\mathsf{q}^q)$, the normalisation $u \cdot (2X + 1/6) = 2Y + X$ together with $C.r = -1/12$, $C.s = -1/2$, $C.t = 1/24$ (constants in the Laurent series field); the base change of $r.\mathrm{curve}$ to $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k_0$ is $C \bullet \mathrm{tateBase}\,k_0\,q$; for each prime $p \mid M'$ and each field $F'$ with a ring map $k_0 \to F'$ and a primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta$, the $p$-component of the $\Gamma_0(M')$-tuple of $r$ becomes, after base change and coefficientwise transport along $f$, the $\mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}$-transform of $\prod_{a} (X - \mathrm{toricPoint}\,F'\,q\,(\zeta^a)_1)$, the product over $1 \le a \le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p \nmid a$; the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point of $r$ becomes the variable change by $C$ of the $\mathrm{LevelPData}$ with $x_P = x_Q = X$ and $y_P = y_Q = Y$; and there exist $P_x, P_y, Q_x, Q_y \in K$ whose Laurent series are the four coordinates of the variable change by $C$ of $\mathrm{cuspData}\,k_0\,q\,(\mathsf{q}^{\ell_g})$ at the vectors $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/q)^2$, and such that the Drinfeld sections $P$ and $Q$ of $r$ pass through $(P_x,P_y)$ and $(Q_x,Q_y)$ respectively in the sense of `IsSectionThrough`.
--
--   Conclusion. For every $d \neq 0$ with $d \mid M'$, every proof `hcK` that $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,(q d)$ lies in $K$, every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ that is an $A_0$-algebra and a $K$-algebra compatibly, every raw representative $x_\Omega$ of $\mathcal{R}$ over $\Omega$ whose class equals the image of $x_0$ under the structure map $K \to \Omega$ (`hxΩ`), and every point $g$ of the affine curve $x_\Omega.\mathrm{curve}$ satisfying `hg`, namely that $g$ has additive order exactly $M'$ and that for each prime $p \mid M'$ and each $n$, whenever $n \cdot g$ is an affine point $(x_1, y_1)$ of additive order $p^{v_p(M')}$ then $x_1$ is a root of the $p$-component of the $\Gamma_0(M')$-tuple of $x_\Omega$, the image of $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,(qd)$ in $\Omega$ equals
--   $$\mathrm{cyclicQuotientJ}\big(x_\Omega.\mathrm{curve},\ \langle (M'/d)\cdot g\rangle,\ d\big),$$
--   that is, $c_4^3/\Delta$ of the cyclic quotient curve of $x_\Omega.\mathrm{curve}$ by the subgroup generated by $(M'/d)\cdot g$ at level $d$.
--
--   This is the Tate-point reading of the $d$-th degeneracy (Atkin–Lehner type) relation for the moduli problem combining $\Gamma_0(M')$-level, a $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-point and Drinfeld level $q$ structure: at the cusp parametrised by the Tate curve with parameter $\mathsf{q}^q$, the $j$-invariant of the quotient by the cyclic subgroup of order $d$ generated by $(M'/d)g$ is the $q$-expansion $j(\mathsf{q}^{qd})$. It is the input to the corresponding statement over the étale chart algebra of the integral model, which transfers the identity from single points to the classifying map of the moduli package.
